Engineering Briefing Board — "The Ship Decides" (library demo)
Third-party material in the film, with its source and licence.

PHOTOGRAPHS (Wikimedia Commons; cut out at the photo's own resolution, graded, and modified as noted)
- Container ship from above (kit object "ship")
  "Containerschiffe auf der Elbe zwischen Wedel und Hamburg" by BAW_Bundesanstalt für Wasserbau,
  CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)
  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Containerschiffe_auf_der_Elbe_zwischen_Wedel_und_Hamburg_(55103130595).jpg
  Changes: rotated, cropped to the hull, background and shadow removed, colour graded.
- Suspension bridge (kit objects "suspension", "print_susp")
  "Bear mountain bridge west II" by Russ, CC BY 2.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/)
  https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bear_mountain_bridge_west_II_(14104379089).jpg
  Changes: cut out, desaturated; the main span between the towers stretched horizontally (×1.7) so the
  towers keep their shape at a two-kilometre span; the main cables (fitted parabolas) and the hangers
  (measured positions) redrawn as clean lines (represents the bridge type, not a real bridge).
The four "print_*" objects are the same photographs, uncropped, on a white photo-paper border, pinned at the
top right of the wall. No real bridge, ship, company or place is named or readable in the film. The licence
trail of every photo looked at is in sources/candidates/candidates.json; kit/tools/fetch_sources.py downloads
the six used ones from Commons.

The whiteboard, its light and ghosts, the frame and tray, magnets, tags, the steel ruler, the cards and sticky
notes, the clearance sketch, the rubber stamp and all marker ink are drawn in code.

